Problem Statement
Nigerian logistics companies lose money and riders at a few predictable points: cash collected on delivery, failed trips, and rider churn. Most of it is tracked by hand today.
• Failed and refused deliveries. Receivers cancel, don't collect, or give wrong numbers or addresses. Trips and fuel are wasted, and COD makes refusing cheap for the receiver.
• COD cash leakage. Riders hold cash all day. Matching it to orders, chasing late remittance and proving what is owed are manual jobs. Jumia's own cashier role, for example, is built around daily reconciliation and chasing riders for unremitted cash.
• Rider churn. Riders move to whoever pays more or work off-app. Companies promise N40,000 to N100,000 a month while riders expect N250,000 to N400,000, so fast and transparent pay matters.
• Trust and disputes. Pilferage, damage and "I never received it" claims have no evidence behind them.
• Input errors. Wrong item descriptions, wrong drop-off numbers and missed notifications cause misdeliveries.
Some problems are not software problems: robbery, road wear, fuel prices, informal taxes and sudden regulation. The app only softens a few of them, in later phases.
The first customers are small and mid-sized logistics companies, including those that deliver for marketplaces such as Jumia. There are three users: the logistics company (the buyer), its riders, and the person receiving the package.
Solution
One product with three surfaces: a company dashboard, a rider app, and WhatsApp for receivers, who install nothing. Every screen is mobile-first and works from phone to desktop, and the product plugs into the current app. Version 1 targets the costliest problems first.
