    Implementation Decisions
    Every decision below is a proposal unless Further Notes says you made it. Build the orders and cash ledger first, because they carry the money problem.
    Modules
    • Accounts and roles. Each logistics company is a separate tenant with its own staff, riders, orders and ledger. Reuse the current app's login, tenants and roles where they exist, and add only what is missing. Roles: owner, manager, dispatcher, cashier, rider. Riders sign in by phone number and a one-time code.
    • Orders and intake. Create by form or CSV. Validate phone format and require an address plus a landmark. Flag duplicates. Mark COD with the amount to collect.
    • Receiver messaging. A WhatsApp conversation per order: confirm, reschedule, cancel, correct address, tracking link, and the delivery code. SMS as a fallback for receivers who cannot get WhatsApp messages.
    • Payments. A pay-by-transfer link per order, backed by a licensed payment partner. The product never holds customer money; the partner settles to the company's own account.
    • Dispatch. Assign confirmed orders to riders, see each rider's cash balance, reassign on failure.
    • Rider app. Assigned jobs, pickup and drop-off photos, delivery code entry, quick messages, failed-delivery reasons, cash balance and remittance.
    • Cash ledger. An append-only record of every cash movement per rider and per order.
    • Dashboard. Success rate, failure reasons, remittance ageing, cash in the field, rider performance.
    • Later modules. Rider payouts and earnings rules, vehicle maintenance log.
    Order lifecycle
    Received, awaiting confirmation, confirmed, assigned, picked up, out for delivery, then one of delivered, failed, cancelled or returned. Dispatch is only allowed from confirmed. Delivered is only allowed with a valid one-time code, or a manager override that is logged with a reason.
    Rules that matter
    • Receiver confirmation gates dispatch. No reply is not consent. After one reminder, the order is flagged for a phone call.
    • Delivery code. A 4-digit code per order, sent to the receiver, single-use, with a limit on wrong attempts. The rider cannot mark delivered without it.
    • Photos. Taken inside the app, not chosen from the gallery, stored with time and order. Kept for a set retention period.
    • Cash cap. Each COD collection adds to the rider's unremitted balance. At or above the company's cap, the rider receives no new assignments until the balance is remitted. The cap is configurable per company and per rider; ₦20,000 is an example used by other platforms, not a recommendation.
    • Remittance matching. A transfer from a rider, or a depot handover recorded by a cashier, reduces the balance and is tied to specific orders. Anything that cannot be matched is queued for a cashier.
    • Paid orders. Once the transfer link is paid, the order is no longer COD and adds nothing to the rider's balance.
    • Corrections. Ledger entries are never edited. A mistake is fixed by a new reversing entry with a reason and the user who made it.
    Data
    Core records: company, user, rider, order, order event, receiver contact, payment, ledger entry, remittance, photo proof. Keep receiver data to what delivery needs, honour opt-out, and set retention rules so the product fits the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023.
    Platform
    • Rider app as a mobile web app built phone first. It works on cheap Android phones, saves actions offline and syncs when the network returns.
    • Company dashboard as a responsive web app that is fully usable on a phone.
    • Stack (follow the current app first; otherwise my default): Next.js with TypeScript, Node, PostgreSQL through Prisma, a background job runner for webhooks and reminders, and object storage for photos.
    • No integration with marketplace systems in version 1. Orders arrive by form or CSV.
    Responsive design
    The entire app is mobile first. Design every screen for a phone, then widen it for tablet and desktop.
    • Every surface. Company dashboard, rider app, receiver pages (tracking and payment) and admin screens all work from 360 px wide upward.
    • Touch. Tap targets at least 44 px, one-handed use for rider flows, and large type for amounts and delivery codes.
    • Tables. On narrow screens, tables become stacked cards. Assigning a rider, recording a remittance and confirming cash are reachable without sideways scrolling.
    • Light on data. Compress photos before upload, load long lists in pages, and stay usable on a slow connection.
    • Receiver pages. Open fast from WhatsApp in a phone browser, with no sign-in and nothing to install.
    Integration with the current app
    Build this as a module inside the current app, not a separate product: same login, same company account, same navigation and same look.
    • Reuse what exists. Authentication, tenants and roles, users and customers, UI components, notifications, and any orders or invoices already stored.
    • Add only what is new. Delivery orders, riders, the cash ledger, receiver messaging and payments.
    • Link, don't copy. If the current app already holds customers or invoices, a delivery order references them.
    • Follow its conventions. Use the current app's stack, database and coding standards. Database changes are additive and must not change how existing features behave.
    • Switch on per company. Existing customers see no change until logistics is turned on for their company.
    • Still to learn. What the current app is built with and what it already provides. See the open questions.
