    Testing Decisions
    Test through one seam: the order lifecycle interface that the dashboard, the rider app and the provider webhooks all call. One seam keeps tests stable while internals change.
    A good test drives the product the way a user or a provider would and checks what comes out: statuses, balances, messages sent, who is allowed to do what. It never checks how the code is organised inside.
    • Fakes at the edges. Replace the WhatsApp provider and the payment partner with fakes that record what was sent and let tests send webhooks back.
    • Behaviours to cover first:
    ◦ An order cannot be dispatched until the receiver confirms.
    ◦ A paid transfer removes the COD debt and the order stops counting toward the rider's cash.
    ◦ A rider at or above the cash cap is excluded from new assignments, and is allowed again once a matched remittance brings the balance under the cap.
    ◦ A delivery completes only with the correct code. Repeated wrong codes lock the attempt, and a used code cannot be reused.
    ◦ A payment webhook delivered twice credits once.
    ◦ Offline rider actions replayed after reconnecting apply once and in order.
    ◦ Ledger entries cannot be edited, only reversed.
    ◦ One company can never read or change another company's orders, riders or ledger.
    ◦ The main flows (create an order, dispatch, record a remittance, complete a delivery) work at phone, tablet and desktop widths without sideways scrolling.
    ◦ Companies that have not switched logistics on see no change, and existing features behave as before.
    • Prior art. Reuse the current app's existing test setup and entry point instead of adding a new one.
    Out of Scope
    Version 1 does not do any of the following.
    • Holding customer money or running wallets or escrow. That needs CBN licensing; a licensed partner handles collection.
    • A marketplace or customer-facing shopping app.
    • Integration with Jumia's or any marketplace's systems.
    • Rider payouts, gamification and bonuses (payouts arrive in version 2).
    • Delivery batching, route optimisation, geofencing, idle alerts and fuel-indexed pricing.
    • Route risk flags and any promise of protection from theft or robbery.
    • Tax, licensing and regulatory compliance tooling, including informal levies.
    • A native iOS or Android app. Riders and the company use web apps.
    • Multi-country support, and any currency other than naira.
    Further Notes
    Validate how cash really moves before building the ledger. Nobody has confirmed that yet, and the ledger shape depends on it.
    What you decided
    • The three users: the logistics company, its riders and the person receiving the package.
    • The receiver is the person getting the package, and their side runs on WhatsApp.
    • You agreed the receiver flow (confirm before dispatch, transfer link, code at the door) is good for the company.
    • The first customers are logistics companies that deliver for Jumia.
    • The problem list in the first section.
    • The whole app must be mobile responsive.
    • It integrates with your current app.
    Everything else here is my proposal: the cash ledger and cap, remittance matching, payouts, phasing, the stack and the testing seam.
